-- Test: section qualification is ADDITIVE.
--
-- `cross-section-qualified.l4` checks that `Section`.Type, `Section`.constructor
-- and `Section`.selector can be WRITTEN (issue #921). This file checks the other
-- half of that claim: giving them a qualified spelling must not change how the
-- BARE name resolves.
--
-- The distinction is not academic. Unqualified references are ranked by section
-- proximity, using a map from each definition's Unique to the section path it
-- was registered at. Constructors and selectors have never had a path recorded,
-- so they behave as top-level: an ancestor of every section. Registering one as
-- part of adding the qualified spelling would silently re-rank every
-- unqualified reference to it — turning working programs ambiguous and
-- ambiguous programs into ones with a binding picked for the user. Whether
-- constructors and selectors SHOULD be section-ranked is a separate question
-- with its own spec; #921 is not the place to answer it.
--
-- Both collisions below are same-typed on purpose: same-typed candidates land
-- in the same proximity group, which is precisely where re-ranking would show.

§ Alpha
DECLARE V IS ONE OF yes, no
DECLARE R HAS w IS A NUMBER

§ Beta

-- a VALUE named like Alpha's constructor, of that very type
GIVETH A V
yes MEANS no

-- a VALUE named like Alpha's selector, of that very selector's type
GIVEN r IS A R
GIVETH A NUMBER
w MEANS 99

§ Gamma

-- Bare `yes`, referenced from a section that is neither Alpha nor Beta, still
-- resolves to Alpha's CONSTRUCTOR (which carries no section path) rather than
-- becoming ambiguous against Beta's same-typed value.
GIVETH A V
useCon MEANS yes

-- Same for the bare selector `w`.
GIVEN r IS A R
GIVETH A NUMBER
useSel MEANS w OF r

-- And Beta's values are still reachable, by qualifying them.
GIVETH A V
qBetaValue MEANS `Beta`.yes

-- ... as are Alpha's constructor and selector, which is what #921 added.
GIVETH A V
qAlphaCon MEANS `Alpha`.yes

qAlphaSel MEANS `Alpha`.w OF (R WITH w IS 7)

#EVAL useCon
#EVAL useSel OF (R WITH w IS 5)
#EVAL qBetaValue
#EVAL qAlphaCon
#EVAL qAlphaSel
